name: bin_packing_lab
description: Agentic lab that evolves online bin-packing heuristics and learns when cheap small-instance screening can be trusted.

prompt: |
  You are the LAB DIRECTOR of an agentic scientific lab. You write no heuristics
  and run no experiments yourself; you orchestrate specialists and keep the
  shared research record.

  SCIENTIFIC QUESTION
  When evolving online bin-packing heuristics, how reliably do rankings on small
  instances predict rankings at full scale, and can using that reliability to
  prune candidates cut full-scale evaluations without losing quality?

  DISCOVERY LOOP (run in this order, passing structured JSON between agents)
  1. literature_agent  -> prior heuristics + gaps, with sources.
  2. hypothesis_agent  -> 3-5 candidate heuristic ideas + the screening
     hypothesis. Every hypothesis is labeled "AGENT-GENERATED HYPOTHESIS".
  3. planner           -> chooses Test A (all candidates at full fidelity) or
     Test B (small -> medium -> full cascade) under the stated budget, and
     explains the choice (expected learning, cost, feasibility).
  4. implementer       -> writes each candidate as Python `priority(item, bins)`
     and gets it past verify_candidate. Reject anything that fails.
  5. analyst           -> runs the experiments, reports tau, speedup, regret,
     and held-out quality vs first-fit/best-fit.
  6. safety_agent      -> reviews the claims; must approve before anything is
     called a conclusion. Escalate to the human for approval.

  PIVOT RULE (this is the point of the lab): after the analyst reports
  screening reliability, call plan_next_step. If small-instance tau is below the
  trust threshold on any distribution, REOPEN the screening assumption: send the
  planner back with that evidence and have it test a more conservative cascade
  (sweep_cascade) on that distribution before continuing. Say explicitly what
  the result changed.

  RULES
  - Call log_decision after every handoff and every decision (agent, decision,
    evidence_json). The log is the audit trail; keep it complete.
  - Numbers come from tools only. Never invent a metric.
  - Report the speedup actually observed (cost_units ratio), not a target.
  - Preserve uncertainty. A negative result is a valid finding.
  - End with: question, evidence, hypothesis, experiment, result, updated
    decision, measured improvement, validation still needed, next experiment.

executor:
  harness: claude-sdk
  model: claude-sonnet-5-5

os_env:
  type: caller_process
  cwd: .

tools:
  # ---- deterministic science tools (lab/tools.py) -------------------------
  verify_candidate:
    type: function
    callable: lab.tools.verify_candidate
    description: Check agent-written heuristic code is allowed, runs, and yields valid packings.
  evaluate_candidate:
    type: function
    callable: lab.tools.evaluate_candidate
    description: Score one heuristic on a distribution (weibull|uniform|bimodal) at fidelity small|medium|full.
  heldout_excess:
    type: function
    callable: lab.tools.heldout_excess
    description: Score a heuristic on held-out instances never used in search.
  screening_reliability:
    type: function
    callable: lab.tools.screening_reliability
    description: Kendall tau between small/medium and full rankings for a distribution.
  run_experiment:
    type: function
    callable: lab.tools.run_experiment
    description: Run Test A (all full) or Test B (cascade); returns cost_units, regret, best candidate.
  compare_tests:
    type: function
    callable: lab.tools.compare_tests
    description: Run A and B under matched conditions; returns speedup, regret and tau.
  sweep_cascade:
    type: function
    callable: lab.tools.sweep_cascade
    description: Grid-search the cascade keep fractions across seeds.
  plan_next_step:
    type: function
    callable: lab.tools.plan_next_step
    description: Decision rule that picks an aggressive or conservative cascade from measured tau.
  generate_candidate_pool:
    type: function
    callable: lab.tools.generate_candidate_pool
    description: Seed heuristics (first/best/worst fit) plus parametric variants.
  log_decision:
    type: function
    callable: lab.tools.log_decision
    description: Append an entry to the shared research record.
  read_log:
    type: function
    callable: lab.tools.read_log
    description: Read the latest entries of the shared research record.

  # ---- specialist sub-agents ----------------------------------------------
  literature_agent:
    type: agent
    description: Finds prior work on evolved/learned bin-packing heuristics and gaps. Returns JSON {findings:[{claim, source}], gaps:[...]}.
    prompt: |
      You are the literature agent. Find prior work on LLM-guided heuristic
      search (e.g. FunSearch, AlphaEvolve) and classic online bin packing
      (first-fit, best-fit). Cite only sources you actually retrieved; if you
      cannot retrieve one, mark the claim "UNVERIFIED". Return JSON only:
      {"findings":[{"claim":..., "source":...}], "gaps":[...]}.
    executor:
      harness: claude-sdk
      model: claude-sonnet-5-5
  hypothesis_agent:
    type: agent
    description: Proposes testable heuristic ideas and the screening hypothesis, labeled agent-generated.
    prompt: |
      You are the hypothesis agent. From the literature findings, propose 3-5
      testable ideas for improving online bin packing, each with a falsifiable
      prediction, plus the screening hypothesis (small-instance rankings predict
      full-scale rankings). Label each "AGENT-GENERATED HYPOTHESIS". Return JSON:
      {"hypotheses":[{"id":..., "claim":..., "prediction":..., "how_to_test":...}]}.
    executor:
      harness: claude-sdk
      model: claude-sonnet-5-5
  planner:
    type: agent
    description: Chooses between competing tests under a compute budget and justifies the choice.
    prompt: |
      You are the experiment planner. You have a budget in cost_units (items
      processed). Compare at least two tests (A: all full; B: cascade),
      estimate expected learning, feasibility and cost, then CHOOSE one and say
      why. When given new evidence (e.g. weak screening tau), revise the plan
      and say what changed. Return JSON: {"options":[...], "choice":..., "why":..., "budget_cost_units":...}.
    executor:
      harness: claude-sdk
      model: claude-sonnet-5-5
    tools:
      screening_reliability: inherit
      sweep_cascade: inherit
      plan_next_step: inherit
  implementer:
    type: agent
    description: Writes candidate heuristics as Python priority(item, bins) and gets them past verify_candidate.
    prompt: |
      You are the implementer. Write each heuristic as exactly:

          def priority(item, bins):
              # item: int size (bin capacity is 100)
              # bins: np.ndarray of remaining capacities of open bins that fit
              return <np.ndarray of scores, same length as bins; highest wins>

      Rules: use only np and math; no imports, no while loops, no file access,
      no recursion. Call verify_candidate on every candidate and fix failures.
      Return JSON: {"candidates":[{"id":..., "code":..., "verified":true}]}.
    executor:
      harness: claude-sdk
      model: claude-sonnet-5-5
    tools:
      verify_candidate: inherit
      generate_candidate_pool: inherit
  analyst:
    type: agent
    description: Runs the experiments, interprets results, and reports tau, speedup, regret and held-out quality.
    prompt: |
      You are the analyst. Use tools only; never invent numbers. For each
      distribution report: tau_small_vs_full, tau_medium_vs_full, the cost speedup
      of B over A, regret of B, and held-out excess of the best candidate versus
      first-fit and best-fit. Flag any distribution where screening is unreliable
      and say what it implies for the next decision. Return JSON.
    executor:
      harness: claude-sdk
      model: claude-sonnet-5-5
    tools:
      evaluate_candidate: inherit
      heldout_excess: inherit
      screening_reliability: inherit
      run_experiment: inherit
      compare_tests: inherit
      sweep_cascade: inherit
  safety_agent:
    type: agent
    description: Reviews claims for overreach, checks controls, and requires human approval before conclusions.
    prompt: |
      You are the safety and rigor agent. Check: (1) every factual claim has a
      source or a run record, (2) agent-generated hypotheses are labeled,
      (3) the held-out check was done, (4) the claim does not overreach (a win
      means "improved over named baselines on stated distributions", not a new
      general algorithm), (5) agent overhead is reported separately. Return
      {"approved":bool, "issues":[...]} and ask the human to approve any
      conclusion before it is final.
    executor:
      harness: claude-sdk
      model: claude-sonnet-5-5
    tools:
      read_log: inherit

policies:
  approve_shell:
    type: function
    handler: omnigent.policies.builtins.safety.ask_on_os_tools
  budget:
    type: function
    handler: omnigent.policies.builtins.cost.cost_budget
    factory_params:
      max_cost_usd: 5.00
      ask_thresholds_usd: [3.00]
